✅ Check. Rows 2 and 3 are twice as tall as rows 0, 1 and 4; row 5 is exactly as tall as its label. The left column is as wide as its widest label.

💡 Try it. Make the window taller and shorter: which rows change their height, and which don’t?

2.3 StackPanel
A StackPanel puts its children one after another, vertically by default or horizontally with Orientation="Horizontal". Spacing adds a gap between them.
&lt;StackPanel Background="Aquamarine"&gt;
    &lt;TextBlock Text="First name"/&gt;
    &lt;TextBox/&gt;
    &lt;TextBlock Text="Last name"/&gt;
    &lt;TextBox Width="100" HorizontalAlignment="Left"/&gt;
&lt;/StackPanel&gt;
In a vertical StackPanel every child is as wide as the panel, unless it sets its own width and alignment like the second TextBox.






TipLab 2.4: StackPanel



🎯 Goal. A StackPanel window with the example above.

Create StackPanelWindow (title StackPanel, 420 × 380) and its launcher button, as in Lab 2.1.
Replace the window’s text with the StackPanel example, and give the panel a Margin="16".

✅ Check. A turquoise block with the two labels and input fields, the second field only 100 pixels wide.
💡 Try it. Set Orientation="Horizontal". What happens to the first TextBox, which has no width of its own? Add Spacing="8".

2.6 Canvas
A Canvas doesn’t arrange anything: you position every child yourself, with the attached properties Canvas.Left, Canvas.Top, Canvas.Right and Canvas.Bottom. It is the only panel that works with coordinates. That makes it right for drawings and diagrams, and wrong for forms: nothing adapts when the window or the text size changes.
&lt;Canvas&gt;
    &lt;TextBlock Canvas.Top="10" Canvas.Left="10" Text="First name"/&gt;
    &lt;TextBox Canvas.Top="30" Canvas.Left="10" Width="200"/&gt;
&lt;/Canvas&gt;






TipLab 2.7: Canvas



🎯 Goal. A small form, positioned by coordinates.

Create CanvasWindow (title Canvas, 360 × 300) and its launcher button.
Replace the window’s text with the Canvas example above; add Margin="16" and Background="AliceBlue" to the canvas.
Add a Last name label and input field yourself, 50 pixels below the first pair.

✅ Check. Both pairs are shown on a light blue area, the input fields 200 pixels wide.
💡 Try it. Make the window very narrow: the input fields are cut off instead of getting smaller.

2.10 Building a UI in code
Everything you write in XAML can also be written in C#: XAML elements are objects, attributes are properties. Attached properties are set with static methods such as DockPanel.SetDock(button, Dock.Top).
var dPanel = new DockPanel();
var btnTop = new Button();
var btnLeft = new Button();

btnTop.Content = "Top Button!";
btnTop.Height = 50;
btnTop.HorizontalAlignment = HorizontalAlignment.Stretch;
btnTop.HorizontalContentAlignment = HorizontalAlignment.Center;
btnTop.VerticalContentAlignment = VerticalAlignment.Center;

btnLeft.Content = "Left Button!";
btnLeft.Width = 95;
btnLeft.VerticalAlignment = VerticalAlignment.Stretch;
btnLeft.HorizontalContentAlignment = HorizontalAlignment.Center;
btnLeft.VerticalContentAlignment = VerticalAlignment.Center;

DockPanel.SetDock(btnTop, Dock.Top);
DockPanel.SetDock(btnLeft, Dock.Left);

dPanel.LastChildFill = false;
dPanel.Children.Add(btnTop);
dPanel.Children.Add(btnLeft);

Content = dPanel;
Keep this for the rare cases where the UI is only known at runtime, for example one button per entry of a list read from a file. For everything else XAML is shorter, easier to read and shows up in the previewer.






TipLab 2.11: A window built in code



🎯 Goal. A window whose content is created in C# only.

Create CodeBehindWindow (title Code-Behind, 360 × 260) and its launcher button.
In CodeBehindWindow.axaml, delete the text Welcome to Avalonia!, so the window has no content.
In CodeBehindWindow.axaml.cs, add using Avalonia.Layout; (for the alignments), and add the code from the section above to the constructor, after InitializeComponent().

✅ Check. A button Top Button! across the top and Left Button! on the left; the rest of the window stays empty because LastChildFill is false.
💡 Try it. Add a third button in code, without a dock, and set LastChildFill to true.

&lt;Style Selector="Button"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontStyle" Value="Italic"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;

&lt;Style Selector="Button.bigText"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;Button Classes="bigText" Content="Create new employee"/&gt;









Selector
Matches




Button
every Button


Button.bigText
every Button with the class bigText


#SaveButton
the element named SaveButton


StackPanel Button
every Button somewhere inside a StackPanel


StackPanel &gt; Button
every Button that is a direct child of a StackPanel




3.1.1 Where styles live
Styles are collected in the Styles property of a control, and apply to that control and everything inside it:

&lt;StackPanel.Styles&gt; (or any other control): only inside that panel,
&lt;Window.Styles&gt;: in this window,
&lt;Application.Styles&gt; in App.axaml: in the whole application. The &lt;FluentTheme/&gt; already there is itself a large collection of styles.

Styles you want to share between several windows can also go into a file of their own and be included with &lt;StyleInclude Source="/Styles/Buttons.axaml"/&gt;.






TipLab 3.1: Style classes



🎯 Goal. A window with a first tab, where a style class makes two of three buttons bigger.

Create the project as in Lab 1.1, named StylesDemo.
In MainWindow.axaml, set Title="Styles Demo" Width="640" Height="420" and replace the text Welcome to Avalonia! with:
&lt;TabControl TabStripPlacement="Left"&gt;
    &lt;TabItem Header="Classes"&gt;
        &lt;StackPanel Margin="16" Spacing="10"&gt;
            &lt;StackPanel.Styles&gt;
                &lt;Style Selector="Button.bigText"&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property="FontStyle" Value="Italic"/&gt;
                    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
                &lt;/Style&gt;
            &lt;/StackPanel.Styles&gt;
            &lt;Button Content="Create new employee"/&gt;
        &lt;/StackPanel&gt;
    &lt;/TabItem&gt;
&lt;/TabControl&gt;
TabStripPlacement="Left" puts the tab headers on the left, so there is room for many tabs.
Add two more buttons below the first: Edit employee and Edit employee activities. Then give the first two buttons the class bigText.

✅ Check. Create new employee and Edit employee are big and italic, Edit employee activities is not.

💡 Try it. Change the selector to Button and watch which buttons change. Then give a TextBlock the class bigText.

3.2 Cascading
When several styles match an element, all of them apply: their setters are merged. If two of them set the same property:

a style whose selector contains a class or pseudo-class beats one with only a type (Button.special beats Button, wherever they are written),
otherwise the style written later wins. There is no “more specific wins” rule as in CSS: TextBlock.caption.small only beats TextBlock.caption if it comes after it.

That makes variants easy. Instead of copying a style, add a second, more specific one that only changes what is different:
&lt;Style Selector="TextBlock.caption"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="30"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,20"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;Style Selector="TextBlock.caption.small"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;TextBlock Classes="caption" Text="A form"/&gt;
&lt;TextBlock Classes="caption small" Text="A smaller form"/&gt;
The second TextBlock matches both styles. It gets the bold weight and the margin from caption, and the font size from caption.small, which comes later.






TipLab 3.2: Cascading



🎯 Goal. A caption and a smaller variant of it, plus a special button that builds on the normal button style.

Add a second tab below the first one:
&lt;TabItem Header="Cascading"&gt;
    &lt;StackPanel Margin="16" Spacing="10"&gt;
        &lt;StackPanel.Styles&gt;
        &lt;/StackPanel.Styles&gt;
    &lt;/StackPanel&gt;
&lt;/TabItem&gt;
Add the two caption styles and the two TextBlocks from the section above to the tab.
Add two buttons, Default button and Special button (with Classes="special"), and write two styles for them yourself:

every Button in this tab: italic, font size 20,
buttons with the class special: additionally Foreground AntiqueWhite and Background DarkSlateBlue.


✅ Check. A smaller form is bold like A form, but smaller. Both buttons are italic and big; only the special one is dark blue.

💡 Try it. Move the caption.small style above the caption style. Then move the Button.special style above the Button style.

3.4 Transitions and animations
Style changes happen instantly. To animate them, give the property a transition: from then on, every change of the property’s value is animated, in both directions.
&lt;Style Selector="TextBox"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="12"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Transitions"&gt;
        &lt;Transitions&gt;
            &lt;DoubleTransition Property="FontSize" Duration="0:0:0.2"/&gt;
        &lt;/Transitions&gt;
    &lt;/Setter&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;Style Selector="TextBox:focus"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="20"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
The text box grows when it gets the focus and shrinks again when it loses it. There are transitions for other property types, too: ColorTransition, BrushTransition, ThicknessTransition, TransformOperationsTransition and more.
For animations with several steps, a style can contain keyframe animations. They run when the style starts to apply:
&lt;Style Selector="Button.fade:pointerover"&gt;
    &lt;Style.Animations&gt;
        &lt;Animation Duration="0:0:0.2" FillMode="Forward"&gt;
            &lt;KeyFrame Cue="0%"&gt;
                &lt;Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1"/&gt;
            &lt;/KeyFrame&gt;
            &lt;KeyFrame Cue="100%"&gt;
                &lt;Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.4"/&gt;
            &lt;/KeyFrame&gt;
        &lt;/Animation&gt;
    &lt;/Style.Animations&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
FillMode="Forward" keeps the value of the last keyframe after the animation has finished.






TipLab 3.3: Pseudo-classes



🎯 Goal. A Save button that turns bold on hover, and a Cancel button that turns italic while pressed.

Add a third tab States, built like the Cascading tab: a StackPanel with Margin="16" Spacing="10" and empty StackPanel.Styles.
Add two buttons: &lt;Button Classes="save" Content="Save"/&gt; and &lt;Button Classes="cancel" Content="Cancel"/&gt;.
Write the two styles yourself: Button.save:pointerover sets FontWeight to Bold, and Button.cancel:pressed sets FontStyle to Italic.

✅ Check. Save is bold only while the mouse is over it. Cancel turns italic only while you hold the mouse button down.
💡 Try it. Remove .save from the selector: now Cancel turns bold on hover, too.








TipLab 3.4: Transitions



🎯 Goal. A text box that grows smoothly when it gets the focus.

Add a fourth tab Animation, built like the others.
Add the TextBox style with the transition from the section above to the tab’s styles, and two text boxes: &lt;TextBox Text="Focus me"/&gt; and &lt;TextBox Text="Or me"/&gt;.
Write the TextBox:focus style yourself, with font size 20.

✅ Check. Click into a text box: it grows smoothly. Click into the other: the first shrinks back while the second grows.
💡 Try it. Add the keyframe example from the section above and a &lt;Button Classes="fade" Content="Hover to fade"/&gt;. Then remove FillMode="Forward".

3.6 Resources
A resource is a named object, stored in the Resources of a control or the application and used with {StaticResource Key}. Colors are the typical example: define them once, use them everywhere.
&lt;Application.Resources&gt;
    &lt;SolidColorBrush x:Key="BrandBrush" Color="DarkSlateBlue"/&gt;
&lt;/Application.Resources&gt;
&lt;Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource BrandBrush}"/&gt;
StaticResource looks the resource up once. DynamicResource keeps watching it, which is needed when the resource can change at runtime, for example when switching between light and dark theme.






TipLab 3.6: An app-wide style



🎯 Goal. Every tab gets a title, styled once for the whole application.

In App.axaml, add the BrandBrush resource from above, and below &lt;FluentTheme /&gt; a style for titles:
&lt;Style Selector="TextBlock.title"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontSize" Value="22"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="SemiBold"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{StaticResource BrandBrush}"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
At the top of each tab’s StackPanel, add a TextBlock with Classes="title" and the tab’s name as text.

✅ Check. All five tabs have a dark blue title, although the style is written only once.
💡 Try it. Change the color of BrandBrush and see all titles change.

3.7 Control templates
A style can only set properties that a control has. To change what a control is made of, you give it a new control template: the tree of elements that draws the control. A template for a button could be a colored Border with a TextBlock inside:
&lt;ControlTemplate x:Key="ButtonTemplate" TargetType="Button"&gt;
    &lt;Border Name="border" BorderBrush="Blue" BorderThickness="3" CornerRadius="3"
            Background="Azure" Padding="3" Margin="3"&gt;
        &lt;TextBlock Margin="{TemplateBinding Padding}" Text="{TemplateBinding Content}"
                   VerticalAlignment="Center" HorizontalAlignment="Center"/&gt;
    &lt;/Border&gt;
&lt;/ControlTemplate&gt;
&lt;Button Template="{StaticResource ButtonTemplate}" Content="Templated button"/&gt;
{TemplateBinding Content} takes the value from the button the template is applied to, so the same template works for every button text.
To style an element inside a template, the selector uses /template/:
&lt;Style Selector="Button:pointerover /template/ Border#border"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Background" Value="LightBlue"/&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
This reads: the Border named border inside the template of a hovered Button.

3.7.1 Styling the parts of the built-in templates
The Fluent theme’s own templates have named parts, too. That matters because some states are drawn by the template, not by the button itself. A style Button:pointerover that sets Background has no visible effect: while hovered, the theme paints the template part PART_ContentPresenter with its own color. To change the hover color, style that part:
&lt;Style Selector="Button.primary"&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Background" Value="DarkSlateBlue"/&gt;
    &lt;Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/&gt;

    &lt;Style Selector="^:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter"&gt;
        &lt;Setter Property="Background" Value="MediumPurple"/&gt;
    &lt;/Style&gt;
&lt;/Style&gt;
The inner style is nested: ^ stands for the selector of the outer style, so the inner selector means Button.primary:pointerover /template/ ContentPresenter#PART_ContentPresenter.
To replace the look of a control type everywhere, Avalonia uses a ControlTheme instead of styles. That goes beyond this chapter.






TipLab 3.7: Control templates



🎯 Goal. A button with a template of its own, and a primary button with its own hover color.

In MainWindow.axaml, add a &lt;Window.Resources&gt; block before the TabControl and put the ButtonTemplate from the section above into it.
Add a sixth tab Templates, with a title as in Lab 3.6, and the button &lt;Button Template="{StaticResource ButtonTemplate}" Content="Templated button"/&gt;.
Add the /template/ style for the hovered border to the tab’s styles.
Add a button with Classes="primary" and the text Primary button, and the nested Button.primary style from the section above.

✅ Check. The templated button is a light blue box with a blue frame; it turns a darker blue on hover. The primary button is dark blue with white text and turns purple on hover.

💡 Try it. Replace the nested hover style by Button.primary:pointerover with a Background setter, and hover again.

4.2 View models and the DataContext
MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel) splits an application into three parts:

the View: the window, its XAML and a minimal code-behind,
the ViewModel: a plain C# class holding the state of the view and the actions on it, as properties and commands. It knows nothing about windows or controls,
the Model: the data and business logic behind it.

The view finds its view model through the DataContext. Set it once, usually in the window’s constructor:
public MainWindow()
{
    InitializeComponent();
    DataContext = new MainWindowViewModel();
}
The DataContext is inherited by every control inside the window. A binding without a # source then refers to a property of the view model: {Binding Name}.
Two more lines belong in every window that uses a view model:
&lt;Window ...
        xmlns:vm="using:HelloMvvm"
        x:DataType="vm:MainWindowViewModel"&gt;

    &lt;Design.DataContext&gt;
        &lt;vm:MainWindowViewModel/&gt;
    &lt;/Design.DataContext&gt;

x:DataType tells the compiler the type of the DataContext. The bindings are then compiled: a misspelled property name is a build error (AVLN2000), not an empty text box at runtime.
Design.DataContext gives the previewer an instance to show, because it can’t run the window’s constructor.







TipLab 4.1: A first view model



🎯 Goal. HelloMvvm with a name input and a greeting, bound to a view model.

Create the project as in Lab 1.1, named HelloMvvm.
Add a class MainWindowViewModel (file MainWindowViewModel.cs) with a string property Name, initially "", and this read-only property:
